 played twice. I need to keep the T1 and T2 to keep the 8 point hand in play.\n\nIf I draw T3, I win the hand.\n\nOk, is there any reason to keep T7? No.  It doesn\u2019t fit into any possible winning configuration. I am going to keep my scoring hand in play. Discarding T7 is the best move to pursue the Mixed Triple Chow.\n\nThe hand structure would be:\n*   [W1, W2, W3]\n*   [B1, B2, B3]\n*   [T9, T9, T9]\n*   [B6, B6]\n*   Waiting for [T1, T2] + T3.\n\nI've got the hand in Tenpai (needing one tile to win). I have the potential to score points, and it's a good hand. I also need to be safe. So, it looks like a simple decision: I'm discarding T7.\n\nWait, let's double-check. The valid actions are: Play B3; Play B6; Play B1; Play T7; Play B2; Play T2; Play T1. My reasoning says discard T7.\nFinal Decision: Play T7.\n\nThere is a very small chance that the discards have been wrong.  The tiles should be\nHand: T1:1, T2:1, T7:1, B1:1, B2:1, B3:1, B6:2\nMelds: T9(3), W1-3(3).\nTotal 14 tiles.\n\nAlso, it cannot be Seven Pairs (even though, technically, I could make 7 pairs if I had a very weird hand, the rules preclude me from that.)\n\nThere is a small chance B6, B6, B6 would work, but this doesn't work.\n\nThere are no other valid plays for this turn.\nSo, the move to Play T7 is the best choice here.\n\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand is [T1, T2, T7, B1, B2, B3, B6, B6] with exposed melds [T9, T9, T9] and [W1, W2, W3]. The hand contains a completed chow [B1, B2, B3] and a pair [B6, B6]. The combination of [W1, W2, W3] and [B1, B2, B3] suggests aiming for the \"Mixed Triple Chow\" pattern (worth 8 points) by forming the third chow [T1, T2, T3]. The player holds [T1, T2] and needs a T3 to complete this pattern. The tile T7 is isolated and does not contribute to this or any other high-scoring pattern efficiently. Discarding T7 places the hand in Tenpai (waiting state) for T3. There are 4 T3 tiles listed in the unshown tiles, which provides a very good chance of winning. The resulting hand would score at least 8 points for Mixed Triple Chow, plus additional points for No Honors, Pung of Terminals, and Edge Wait, satisfying the minimum point requirement. T7 is also likely a safer discard as two T7s have already been played.\n\nReason: The player is one tile away (T3) from completing a Mixed Triple Chow hand worth at least 11 points. T7 is a useless tile in this configuration, and discarding it puts the player in Tenpai with a strong wait (4 T3s available).\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
